---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { contact, hrefs } from "../config/site";

/* ============================================================
   JVG SHIPPING PROCESS — FAIR PLAY GUARANTEE variant.
   noIndex hand-out link (see jvg-shipping-process.astro notes).
   Differences from the base page: payment is sent after the
   same-day arrival inspection (not at the FedEx counter), there
   is a 6th step, and the Fair-Play Guarantee section spells out
   the renegotiate-or-return terms. Keep the FedEx account number
   and Fraser Drive shipping address EXACTLY as on live.
   ============================================================ */

const SHIP_TO = "47 North Fraser Drive East, Mesa, AZ 85203";
const FEDEX_ACCT = "541950800";
---

<Layout
  title="JVG Shipping Process - Fair Play Guarantee"
  description="Step-by-step instructions for shipping your guitar to Joe's Vintage Guitars, backed by the Fair-Play Guarantee: free insured shipping, same-day inspection, and same-day payment."
  canonical="/jvg-shipping-process-2/"
  noIndex={true}
>
  <section class="ship-title-bar">
    <div class="ship-inner"><h1>JVG Shipping Process</h1><p class="ship-sub">Backed by the Fair-Play Guarantee</p></div>
  </section>

  <section class="ship-body">
    <div class="ship-inner">
      <p class="ship-lede">
        Selling your guitar to me from out of state is simple, secure, and backed by a fair-play guarantee. Your
        guitar ships fully insured, is inspected the same day it arrives, and payment goes out to you immediately
        after. If anything unexpected turns up during inspection, you&rsquo;re still protected. More on that below.
      </p>

      <h2>The Process at a Glance</h2>
      <p>Six simple steps. Your part is done once the guitar is at FedEx; everything after that is on me.</p>
      <div class="ship-callout">
        <ul>
          <li>I cover all packing and shipping costs. Nothing out of your pocket.</li>
          <li>Your guitar is fully insured in transit under our private Heritage Musical Instrument policy, better coverage than FedEx offers.</li>
          <li>Your guitar is inspected the same day it arrives, and payment is sent immediately after.</li>
          <li>If the guitar&rsquo;s originality or condition differs from what we discussed, we renegotiate fairly, or I ship it back at my cost with a free professional setup, free photographs, and a signed authentication letter.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2><span class="ship-step">Step 1</span> Send Me Your Info</h2>
      <p>
        Text or email me a photo of your driver&rsquo;s license along with your preferred email address. The
        license photo is strictly for fraud prevention; it confirms the sale on both sides and protects both of
        us. Your information is kept private and is never shared.
      </p>

      <h2><span class="ship-step">Step 2</span> Pack the Inside of the Case</h2>
      <p>
        This is the single most important thing you&rsquo;ll do. Before heading to FedEx, open the case and fill
        every bit of empty space so the guitar cannot move at all inside it. Use soft materials you have on hand:
        newspaper, old t-shirts, towels, paper towels, or foam. Close the case and shake it vigorously in every
        direction. If you hear or feel any shifting, add more padding.
      </p>
      <div class="ship-warn">
        <strong>Do not use bubble wrap inside the case.</strong> Bubble wrap can react with the nitrocellulose
        lacquer used on vintage guitar finishes and leave permanent marks or stick to the finish over time. Soft
        cloth, newspaper, and paper towels are all safe. Bubble wrap is fine on the outside of the case, just not
        touching the guitar.
      </div>
      <p>
        Why this matters: a loose guitar inside a case is the number-one cause of shipping damage. Even with
        perfect outer packing, a guitar rattling around inside its own case can crack a headstock or dent the
        body. Two minutes of stuffing t-shirts around the neck and body prevents almost every shipping issue
        I&rsquo;ve ever seen.
      </p>

      <h2><span class="ship-step">Step 3</span> Take It to FedEx</h2>
      <p>
        This needs to be a genuine <strong>FedEx Print &amp; Ship Center</strong>, not a third-party
        pack-and-ship store that carries a FedEx sign. The real locations have the staff, materials, and
        guitar-sized boxes I need them to use. It&rsquo;s a good idea to call ahead and ask if they have a guitar
        box in stock; most do, but a quick call saves a wasted trip. Phone tip: when the automated menu picks up,
        just press 7 and it puts you straight through to a staff member at that location.
      </p>
      <p>
        If there isn&rsquo;t an official FedEx Print &amp; Ship Center near you, let me know before you head out
        and I&rsquo;ll arrange a pickup or an alternative for you.
      </p>

      <h2><span class="ship-step">Step 4</span> At the Counter</h2>
      <p>
        Snap a photo of the guitar on the counter and text it to me; that&rsquo;s my signal that everything is on
        track. Then hand the guitar to a FedEx employee, ask them to pack it very tightly (no movement inside the
        box when shaken), and give them this information:
      </p>
      <table class="ship-table">
        <tbody>
          <tr><th>FedEx Account #</th><td>{FEDEX_ACCT}</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Charge to Account</th><td>Packaging + Shipping (you won&rsquo;t pay a cent)</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Ship To</th><td>{SHIP_TO}</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Return Address</th><td>Your own address</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Shipping Speed</th><td>Cheapest option, unless I&rsquo;ve told you otherwise</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Insurance</th><td>None. See the note below.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="ship-note">
        About insurance: we decline FedEx&rsquo;s insurance on purpose. Every guitar we buy is covered under our
        private Heritage Musical Instrument insurance policy, which offers broader coverage and a much smoother
        claims process than FedEx&rsquo;s shipping insurance. Your guitar is fully insured from the moment it
        leaves your hands until it arrives at my shop.
      </p>

      <h2><span class="ship-step">Step 5</span> Scan, Drop Off, and You&rsquo;re Done</h2>
      <p>
        After the guitar is packed and labeled, have the FedEx employee scan in the label and hand you the
        drop-off receipt. Take a photo of that receipt and text it to me. That&rsquo;s it, your part is done. The
        guitar is now insured, tracked, and on its way. You can watch the tracking number, or just wait for my
        text when it arrives.
      </p>

      <h2><span class="ship-step">Step 6</span> Arrival, Inspection, Payment</h2>
      <p>
        The moment your guitar arrives at my shop, I inspect it the same day. No sitting around for a week
        waiting for a dealer to &ldquo;get to it.&rdquo; If the guitar matches what we discussed, payment is sent
        to you the same day via your preferred method. Most sellers have the money in their account within hours
        of the guitar hitting my shop.
      </p>

      <h2>Our Fair-Play Guarantee</h2>
      <p>
        Vintage guitars have long histories. Sometimes a pickup was swapped decades ago, a tuner was replaced, or
        a finish was touched up, and sometimes even the current owner doesn&rsquo;t know. If the guitar&rsquo;s
        condition or originality differs from what we discussed, here&rsquo;s exactly how it&rsquo;s handled:
        I&rsquo;ll call you the same day to walk through what I found, explain how it affects value, and propose
        a fair revised price. From there, you have two choices:
      </p>
      <div class="ship-callout">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Accept the new price.</strong> Payment sent the same day, transaction complete. No hassle, no delays.</li>
          <li><strong>Take the guitar back.</strong> I ship it back to you at my cost, fully insured, along with everything listed below.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <p>If you choose to take the guitar back, it comes home with:</p>
      <ul class="ship-check">
        <li>A complimentary professional setup: intonation, action, and neck relief dialed in by a working luthier.</li>
        <li>A set of free professional photographs of the instrument, for insurance, a future sale, or your own records.</li>
        <li>A free written and signed authentication letter detailing the guitar&rsquo;s originality, modifications, and historical context. The same kind of letter normally costs hundreds of dollars from an outside appraiser.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        You lose nothing. Most sellers who go this route end up with a better-playing, better-documented guitar
        than they sent, at zero cost to them.
      </p>

      <h2>Payment Options</h2>
      <p>
        I keep payment flexible so you can use whatever you already trust: PayPal, Venmo, Zelle, Cash App, wire
        transfer, or a mailed check.
      </p>
      <p class="ship-note">
        One quick note on limits: some apps (Venmo, Zelle, Cash App, PayPal) have daily or per-transfer limits
        depending on your account. For higher-dollar sales, let me know your preferred payment method in advance
        so we can confirm it&rsquo;ll go through in one shot, or plan a quick wire transfer instead.
      </p>

      <h2>Quick Reference Checklist</h2>
      <ul class="ship-check">
        <li>Sent email address and driver&rsquo;s license photo</li>
        <li>Packed the inside of the case tightly, soft materials only, no bubble wrap</li>
        <li>Called ahead about a guitar box (press 7 if you get the robot)</li>
        <li>Located an official FedEx Print &amp; Ship Center (not a third party)</li>
        <li>Texted a photo of the guitar on the counter</li>
        <li>Gave FedEx account #{FEDEX_ACCT} and the Mesa, AZ address</li>
        <li>Chose the cheapest shipping option (unless I specified otherwise)</li>
        <li>Declined FedEx insurance</li>
        <li>Texted a photo of the drop-off receipt</li>
        <li>Confirmed preferred payment method in advance</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="ship-contact">
        <p><strong>Questions? Text me anytime.</strong></p>
        <p>
          If anything feels unclear before, during, or after your trip to FedEx, reach out. I&rsquo;d rather
          answer ten questions than have you guess on anything. This process has worked smoothly for hundreds of
          sellers across the country, and I&rsquo;ll make sure it works smoothly for you.
        </p>
        <p>
          Phone/Text: <a href={hrefs.tel}>{contact.phone}</a><br />
          Email: <a href={hrefs.mailto}>{contact.email}</a><br />
          Shipping Address: {SHIP_TO}<br />
          FedEx Account #: {FEDEX_ACCT}
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .ship-inner { max-width: 820px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 24px; }
  .ship-title-bar {
    background: var(--color-brand-rust-dark, #682412);
    padding: 180px 24px 48px;
    text-align: center;
  }
  .ship-title-bar h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(32px, 5vw, 54px);
    color: var(--color-brand-cream);
    line-height: 1.1;
  }
  .ship-sub {
    margin: 10px 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(16px, 2.2vw, 22px);
    color: var(--color-brand-brown-tan);
  }
  .ship-body { background: #fff; padding: 44px 0 72px; }
  .ship-body p, .ship-body li {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-brand-charcoal);
  }
  .ship-body p { margin: 0 0 16px; }
  .ship-lede { font-size: 18px !important; }
  .ship-note { font-style: italic; }
  .ship-body a { color: var(--color-brand-rust); text-decoration: underline; font-weight: 600; }
  .ship-body h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 24px;
    color: var(--color-brand-rust-dark);
    margin: 36px 0 12px;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 12px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  .ship-step {
    display: inline-block;
    background: var(--color-brand-rust-bright, #be4b25);
    color: #fff;
    font-size: 14px;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    padding: 4px 12px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .ship-callout {
    background: var(--color-brand-cream-light, #f9f1de);
    border-left: 4px solid var(--color-brand-rust-bright);
    padding: 16px 20px;
    margin: 0 0 16px;
  }
  .ship-callout ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; }
  .ship-callout li { margin-bottom: 6px; }
  .ship-warn {
    background: #f6e0da;
    border-left: 4px solid #842012;
    padding: 14px 18px;
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: #5a1a0d;
  }
  .ship-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 0 0 18px;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 15px;
  }
  .ship-table th, .ship-table td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 11px 14px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(62, 42, 20, 0.12);
    color: var(--color-brand-charcoal);
    vertical-align: top;
  }
  .ship-table th {
    width: 36%;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-brand-rust-dark);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .ship-check { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 18px; padding: 0; }
  .ship-check li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 30px;
    margin-bottom: 9px;
  }
  .ship-check li::before {
    content: "✓";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    color: var(--color-brand-rust-bright);
    font-weight: 700;
  }
  .ship-contact {
    margin-top: 36px;
    background: var(--color-brand-cream, #eedfc0);
    padding: 20px 24px;
    border-radius: 6px;
  }
  .ship-contact p { margin: 0 0 8px; }
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .ship-title-bar { padding: 120px 16px 36px; }
    .ship-table th { white-space: normal; width: 40%; }
  }
</style>
